{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "expand-sourcetype-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d, the SOURCE TYPE dimension at width five: the `expand-sourcetype` run computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE through `moves-onroad.esm`. Source types 11 (motorcycle), 21 (passenger car), 31 (passenger truck), 51 (refuse truck) and 62 (combination long-haul truck) -- two light-duty, one heavy-duty vocational, one long-haul and a motorcycle, which is as far apart as this database's physics gets.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES over `expand-fueltype-diesel`, which is the same axis at width two.\n\n* FORTY DRIVE CYCLES RATHER THAN FOUR, and forty that disagree. A drive cycle is per VEHICLE, and a vehicle is a row of `sourceusetypephysics` -- (sourceTypeID, regClassID, model-year range), with its own rolling, rotating and drag terms, its own mass and its own fixed mass factor. These five source types select 40 of the table's 178 rows: 1 each for the motorcycle and the passenger car, 3 for the passenger truck, 15 for the refuse truck and 20 for the combination long-haul truck. So the vehicle-specific power of a drive-schedule second, the operating mode it bins into and the mode distribution the rates are weighted by differ 40 ways, and `n_physicsRow` is 40 where `expand-fueltype-diesel` needs 4 and every run in `runspecs/` before them needed 1. A motorcycle and a combination long-haul truck do not share an operating-mode distribution, and neither do a refuse truck's fifteen regulatory-class-and-model-year bands.\n\n* A COHORT COUNT THAT IS A SUM OVER FIVE UNEQUAL TERMS. 430 rows: 41 + 125 + 125 + 89 + 50. Source type 11 carries one fuel and 41 model years; 21 and 31 carry four fuels each and reach the full 125; 51 and 62 carry fuels the database has fewer sample vehicles for. 656 candidates in the grid, 430 survivors, and no declaration anywhere says 430 -- `cohortSurvivorCount` recomputes it and `tools/check-runspec-shape.py` holds the emitted relation to it.\n\nTHE TEN SELECTIONS, ONE BINDING AND THE ORACLE are everything this document carries, and it differs from `runspecs/expand-fueltype-diesel.esm` in eleven `url_template`s alone.\n\nWHY THIS DOCUMENT IS IN `runspecs/pending/` AND NOT `runspecs/`, as of 2026-09-18. The MODEL reason is gone: the drive cycle became per vehicle in PLAN.md §7.3.7, `expand-fueltype-diesel` moved to `runspecs/` on the strength of it, and this document validates and evaluates as far as it is allowed to. What stops it is MEMORY, and the number is measured rather than guessed: evaluating it peaks at 32.0 GB of resident memory against a 40 GB cgroup limit that counts page cache too, and the kernel kills it. It is not the drive cycle -- §7.3.7 LOWERED this run's peak from 34.4 GB and halved its wall clock -- and it is not the source-bin padding, which is flat from 8 slots to 2 (32.0, 33.2 GB) and drops only at 1 (24.3 GB). `expand-fueltype-diesel` peaks at 24.6 GB and runs, so the ceiling sits between them and nothing in this document can move it.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY PROVEN BY RUNNING IT, which is why it is checked in rather than deleted. It is the width-five companion to `runspecs/expand-fueltype-diesel.esm`, which is the same claim at width two and which now reproduces all 248 of its rows at a worst cell of 7.372e-06 -- both regulatory classes of source type 32, the split at model year 2010, and all four fuels. This document exists so that the step which makes the chain fit in memory has a run to widen to, rather than a run to write afterwards, and so that the claim being made about the drive cycle is stated at a width where a refuse truck's fifteen vehicles and a long-haul truck's twenty are on the axis.\n\nWHAT IT WAITS ON, and it is upstream rather than in this tree: EarthSciML/EarthSciAST#418, finding F58. A join-gated `faq` retains about 80 bytes of resident memory per CANDIDATE JOIN PAIR and another 78 per fact-table row, in a thread-local cache that never evicts, so peak memory tracks the join's match set rather than the arrays a document declares. This run declares 0.31 GiB of arrays across 462 variables and is KILLED at 32.0 GB -- where it died, not what it needs. PR #420 fixes #418 and was measured downstream on 2026-09-19: 3x less memory and 3-4x less wall clock on this model with byte-identical answers, and this run still does not fit, reaching 38.67 GB and holding flat there for 70 seconds before the kill. It is now just over the cap rather than three times over. The measurement that found it is in PLAN.md §7.3.7 -- an RSS timeline of `expand-fueltype-diesel` that is three steps of 4.2 GB, each followed by 21 seconds of allocation-free compute and none of it released -- and `docs/findings/F58_a_join_gate_retains_its_match_set.esm` is the minimal case, with four metaparameters that separate the two terms. The fix is priced upstream at 12 to 16 bytes a pair, which would put this run under 10 GB. Nothing in this document can move it, and nothing else in this tree is blocked on it.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "expand-day"
    ]
  },
